    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Modified_Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    nf = 7\n    nc = 5\n    facility_coords = [(2, 88), (75, 55), (98, 0), (32, 51), (0, 70), (78, 2), (36, 65)]\n    customer_coords = [(92, 30), (29, 63), (74, 36), (5, 35), (21, 44)]\n    capacities = [191.0, 172.0, 168.0, 77.0, 94.0, 157.0, 199.0]\n    demands = [48.0, 47.0, 17.0, 37.0, 35.0]\n    fixed_costs = [377.0, 293.0, 287.0, 261.0, 346.0, 246.0, 328.0]\n    rate = 0.5\n\n    # Compute distances\n    dist = {}\n    for i in range(nf):\n        for j in range(nc):\n            dx = facility_coords[i][0] - customer_coords[j][0]\n            dy = facility_coords[i][1] - customer_coords[j][1]\n            dist[(i,j)] = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)\n\n    # Variables\n    open_f = {i: m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f'Open_F{i}') for i in range(nf)}\n    ship = {(i,j): m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f'Ship_F{i}_C{j}')\n            for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc)}\n\n    # Objective\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf))\n    obj += quicksum(dist[(i,j)]*rate*ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc))\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction\n    for j in range(nc):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf)) >= demands[j])\n\n    # Capacity linking\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= capacities[i]*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: min throughput 4 per open facility\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) >= 4.0*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: F4 requires F1\n    m.addConstr(open_f[4] <= open_f[1])\n\n    # Policy: at most 6 facilities open\n    m.addConstr(quicksum(open_f[i] for i in range(nf)) <= 6)\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Executive Leadership Team\nFrom: Supply Chain Consultant\nSubject: Distribution Network Opening and Shipment Allocation Decision\nDate: [Today]\n\nExecutive summary\nWe are preparing a network decision that determines which distribution centers to open and how to allocate shipments from open sites to customers. The aim is to meet every customer\u2019s needs while minimizing the total landed cost, defined as the sum of facility opening costs and distance-based transportation spend, all within our operating capacities and company policies.\n\nDecision to be made\n- Select the subset of candidate distribution centers to activate.\n- Assign each customer\u2019s demand to one or more open facilities and define shipment flows.\n- Produce a costed plan that respects facility capacity limits and company policies, and meets all customer service requirements.\n\nOperating requirements and company policies\n- Customer service: Every customer\u2019s demand is to be fully satisfied. Orders may be sourced from any open distribution center, provided total allocations meet the customer\u2019s requirement.\n- Facility activation: Shipments may originate only from facilities that are opened.\n- Capacity discipline: Each facility has a finite throughput limit; total outbound volume assigned to a facility must not exceed its available capacity.\n- Cost model: Opening a facility incurs a fixed operating cost. Transportation spend scales with distance and shipped volume at a uniform per\u2011kilometer rate.\n- Minimum utilization rule: To justify operating overhead, every opened facility must handle at least 4 units of total outbound shipments.\n- Hub dependency: The site labeled F4 relies on the regional hub labeled F1. If F4 is activated, F1 must also be activated.\n- Oversight limit: Due to management oversight capacity, the company will operate no more than 6 distribution centers at any point in time.\n\nPlanning approach and outputs\n- We will evaluate candidate sites and customer locations using their provided map coordinates to estimate shipping distances and costs.\n- The analysis will balance fixed opening costs against transportation savings from proximity and capacity availability.\n- The result will include:\n  - The recommended set of distribution centers to open.\n  - Customer-to-facility shipment plans showing volumes by lane.\n  - Facility utilizations confirming capacity adherence and minimum throughput compliance.\n  - An all\u2011in annualized cost estimate and sensitivity commentary on key assumptions.\n\nAssumptions and governance\n- Distances will be applied consistently across all facility\u2013customer pairs, and transportation charges will reflect the uniform rate structure.\n- Policies listed above are mandatory and will be enforced in all scenarios.\n- Any changes to demand forecasts, capacities, or cost inputs will trigger a refreshed run and updated recommendations.\n\nNext steps\n- Confirm the planning data in the annex.\n- Align on any service-level nuances (e.g., allowable multi\u2011sourcing and fulfillment priorities).\n- Run the decision model and review the recommended configuration with Finance and Operations for sign\u2011off.\n\nAnnex: Planning data\n- Facility coordinates: Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (2, 88)\n  - F1: (75, 55)\n  - F2: (98, 0)\n  - F3: (32, 51)\n  - F4: (0, 70)\n  - F5: (78, 2)\n  - F6: (36, 65)\n- Customer coordinates: Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (92, 30)\n  - C1: (29, 63)\n  - C2: (74, 36)\n  - C3: (5, 35)\n  - C4: (21, 44)\n- Facility capacities: [191.0, 172.0, 168.0, 77.0, 94.0, 157.0, 199.0]\n- Customer demands: [48.0, 47.0, 17.0, 37.0, 35.0]\n- Fixed opening costs by facility: [377.0, 293.0, 287.0, 261.0, 346.0, 246.0, 328.0]\n- Transport rate per kilometer per unit: 0.5",
    "LLM_description_template": "To: Executive Leadership Team\nFrom: Supply Chain Consultant\nSubject: Distribution Network Opening and Shipment Allocation Decision\nDate: [Today]\n\nExecutive summary\nWe are preparing a network decision that determines which distribution centers to open and how to allocate shipments from open sites to customers. The aim is to meet every customer\u2019s needs while minimizing the total landed cost, defined as the sum of facility opening costs and distance-based transportation spend, all within our operating capacities and company policies.\n\nDecision to be made\n- Select the subset of candidate distribution centers to activate.\n- Assign each customer\u2019s demand to one or more open facilities and define shipment flows.\n- Produce a costed plan that respects facility capacity limits and company policies, and meets all customer service requirements.\n\nOperating requirements and company policies\n- Customer service: Every customer\u2019s demand is to be fully satisfied. Orders may be sourced from any open distribution center, provided total allocations meet the customer\u2019s requirement.\n- Facility activation: Shipments may originate only from facilities that are opened.\n- Capacity discipline: Each facility has a finite throughput limit; total outbound volume assigned to a facility must not exceed its available capacity.\n- Cost model: Opening a facility incurs a fixed operating cost. Transportation spend scales with distance and shipped volume at a uniform per\u2011kilometer rate.\n- Minimum utilization rule: To justify operating overhead, every opened facility must handle at least 4 units of total outbound shipments.\n- Hub dependency: The site labeled F4 relies on the regional hub labeled F1. If F4 is activated, F1 must also be activated.\n- Oversight limit: Due to management oversight capacity, the company will operate no more than 6 distribution centers at any point in time.\n\nPlanning approach and outputs\n- We will evaluate candidate sites and customer locations using their provided map coordinates to estimate shipping distances and costs.\n- The analysis will balance fixed opening costs against transportation savings from proximity and capacity availability.\n- The result will include:\n  - The recommended set of distribution centers to open.\n  - Customer-to-facility shipment plans showing volumes by lane.\n  - Facility utilizations confirming capacity adherence and minimum throughput compliance.\n  - An all\u2011in annualized cost estimate and sensitivity commentary on key assumptions.\n\nAssumptions and governance\n- Distances will be applied consistently across all facility\u2013customer pairs, and transportation charges will reflect the uniform rate structure.\n- Policies listed above are mandatory and will be enforced in all scenarios.\n- Any changes to demand forecasts, capacities, or cost inputs will trigger a refreshed run and updated recommendations.\n\nNext steps\n- Confirm the planning data in the annex.\n- Align on any service-level nuances (e.g., allowable multi\u2011sourcing and fulfillment priorities).\n- Run the decision model and review the recommended configuration with Finance and Operations for sign\u2011off.\n\nAnnex: Planning data\n- Facility coordinates: {FACILITY_COORDINATES}\n- Customer coordinates: {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}\n- Facility capacities: {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}\n- Customer demands: {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}\n- Fixed opening costs by facility: {FIXED_COSTS}\n- Transport rate per kilometer per unit: {TRANSPORT_RATE}"
